'''back<sup>1</sup>''' (bak), ''a.'' [< ''back''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', and ''back''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.'', the attributive use of the noun, as in ''backbone,'' mingling with that of the adv., as in ''back yard,'' the yard which is back, ''back spring,'' a spring backward, etc. As with ''fore, hind, after,'' etc., there is no definite dividing line between the separate adj. use and the use in composition.] '''1.''' Lying or being behind; opposite to the front; hinder; rear: as, the ''back'' part of anything; a ''back'' door or window; ''back'' stairs; the ''back'' side of a field.
{{smaller block|I will take away mine hand, and thou shalt see my ''back'' parts: but my face shall not be seen.
{{right|Ex. xxxiii. 23.}}}}
Hence—'''2.''' Away from the front position or rank; remote in place or condition; far in the rear, literally or figuratively: as, the ''back'' settlements of a country.
{{smaller block|In December we had two insurrections of the ''back'' inhabitants of our province.
{{right|''Franklin,'' Autobiog., p. 315.}}}}
'''3.''' In a backward direction; returning in the direction whence it came: as, a ''back'' stroke;

