'''back<sup>1</sup>''' (bak), ''adv.'' [By apheresis for ''aback'', < ME. ''abak'', < AS. ''on bæc'': see ''aback'' and ''back''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' To or toward the rear; backward; in the reverse direction: as, to step or shrink ''back''; the tide flowed ''back''.
{{smaller block|All shrank ''back'' aghast, and left the denouncer of woe standing alone in the centre of the hall.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 23.}}}}
'''2.''' From forward motion or progress; from advancing or advancement; in a state of restraint, hindrance, or retardation: with such verbs as ''keep'' and ''hold'': as, he was held ''back'' with difficulty; the police kept ''back'' the crowd.
{{smaller block|The Lord hath kept thee ''back'' from honour.
{{right|Num. xxiv. 11.}}}}
'''3.''' To or toward one’s (its or their) original starting-point, place, or condition: as, to go ''back'' to the city, to one’s old occupation, to one’s former belief.
{{smaller block|<poem>I must bear answer ''back''
How you excuse my brother. {{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, iv. 3.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|Each successive wave rushes forward, breaks, and rolls ''back''. {{right|''Macaulay'', Sir J. Mackintosh.}}}}
'''4.''' From a present, usual, or natural position; in a direction opposite to some other, expressed
